{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Laban said, It is good, I will give her, etc.</b> One needs to know for what purpose Laban said this. Also, how can it be justified to say <b>It is good I will give her to you rather than I will give her, etc.</b> if no one else will work — or perhaps Laban lied in this and hinted to him that there is another who would likewise work for Rachel. And further it is difficult: how can it be justified to say ״I will give her״ in the language of a gift, since he is working for her seven years, which is equivalent to her value — for do you not know that one who acquires a Hebrew slave [works and] he works six years, and here he gave his equivalent in exchange for Rachel's equivalent? One further needs to understand his saying <b>dwell with me</b>, which is superfluous language." "<b>Indeed,</b> his evil intention is discernible from within his words, for he intended to deceive in order to give him Leah, as he did afterward. And for this reason he prefaced by speaking to him with these words. And this is the measure of his words: <b>It is good, I will give her to you</b> — he said this in the language of a gift in order to preclude [the claim] that this is a sale, so that he could not ultimately say to him, ״her have I acquired from you,״ as indeed he said: ״Is it not for Rachel that I served, etc.״ (Genesis 29:25), and as I will explain there with the help of HaShem. And he was concerned that Jacob might understand that he was giving her as an outright gift, and ultimately he would come against him with the claim of ״give me my wages״ for the seven years he had worked. Therefore he said that the labor he works with him shall be in exchange for what he prefers in him over another man — and this is the measure of the verse: <b>It is good, I will give her to you</b> — and I choose you on account of the fact that you work with me — <b>rather than I will give her to another man</b> without labor; and in no case is this Rachel's monetary value, nor is it in the category of a sale that he brings her in." "And his saying <b>dwell with me</b> stipulates upon him that he must dwell with him and not wander afar and work from a distance, but rather with him he must dwell the entire time of the labor, so that his eyes remain watchful over his labor. Further, he cunningly contrived to say to him to dwell with him forever, just as Jethro stipulated with Moses (Exodus Rabbah 1:33) and did not need to make him swear on the matter, because these are things acquired through speech. And Jethro, who needed to make Moses swear, intended to obligate him even if Tziporah would wish to go — he made him swear on his own terms that [Moses] would remain with the man. And this claim he had in mind to raise against Jacob when necessary, and for this reason HaShem commanded him (Genesis 31:24): ״Take heed lest you speak with Jacob״ — and Jacob took no precaution against this, for Laban's words were spoken to him in a friendly manner, along these lines: And to whom would I give my daughter better than you? Sit, my son, with me — meaning, for we are kinsmen, men, one to another. But his heart did not think so."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:54.189897268Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}